 "beats": [
  {
   "id": "o1",
   "text": "He was the highest-paid Black entertainer in America. And every night, before he walked on stage, he had to paint his face black.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "suit1921",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.22
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.05
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.36
    ]
   },
   "overlays": {
    "hook": "America's highest-paid Black star. *Every night, he had to black up.*"
   },
   "captions": false
  },
  {
   "id": "o2",
   "text": "He was the first Black man to star on Broadway, and the only Black star in the biggest white show of its day.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "pk4",
    "focus": [
     0.7,
     0.3
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "o3",
   "text": "W. C. Fields called him the funniest man he ever saw, and the saddest man he ever knew.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "pk1",
    "focus": [
     0.7,
     0.3
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "o4",
   "text": "His name was Bert Williams. This is his story.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Bert Williams",
    "sub": "1874 to 1922"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "e1",
   "text": "Egbert Austin Williams was born on November 12th, 1874, in Nassau, in the Bahamas.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "young1896",
    "caption": "Bert Williams as a young man"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "pin": {
     "label": "Nassau, Bahamas"
    }
   },
   "section": "early"
  },
  {
   "id": "e2",
   "text": "His family moved to the United States while he was a boy, and he grew up partly in California.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "California",
    "sub": "a long way from Broadway"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "e3",
   "text": "As a teenager, he joined traveling minstrel shows. In 1893, in San Francisco, he met another young performer, George Walker.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "ww",
    "caption": "Bert Williams and George Walker"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "1893"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "e4",
   "text": "They teamed up. Walker was the flashy one, a strutting dandy. Bert was the slow, sad-faced one, who always got the worst of it.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "jonah",
    "caption": "Williams and Walker"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "e5",
   "text": "In 1896 they reached New York.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "bar0",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "1896"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "m1",
   "text": "There was a catch. In those days, Black performers were expected to wear blackface, just like the white minstrels who mocked them.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "cork",
    "focus": [
     0.4,
     0.4
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.05,
     1.15
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.45,
     0.5
    ]
   },
   "section": "mask"
  },
  {
   "id": "m2",
   "text": "Bert was light-skinned. So before every show, he burned cork, and smeared it over his face.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "bowtie",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.15
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.05,
     1.15
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "m3",
   "text": "Then he walked out on stage, and played the slow, unlucky fool the crowds expected.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "wave",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.15
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.05,
     1.15
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "m4",
   "text": "Williams and Walker even billed themselves as Two Real Coons, to set themselves apart from the white men in burnt cork.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "coons",
    "caption": "Williams and Walker poster, 1903"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "m5",
   "text": "Off stage, they dressed in sharp suits, and posed for elegant photographs. The contrast was the point.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "young1902",
    "caption": "Bert Williams, 1902"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "m6",
   "text": "In 1901, they recorded for the Victor company. They were among the very first Black artists to record for a major label.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "1901",
    "sub": "Victor records"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "b1",
   "text": "In 1903, they opened In Dahomey, a musical written, directed and performed by an all-Black cast.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "dahomey",
    "caption": "In Dahomey, 1903"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "1903"
   },
   "section": "broadway"
  },
  {
   "id": "b2",
   "text": "It was the first all-Black musical to play a major Broadway theater.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "cakewalk",
    "caption": "In Dahomey: the cakewalk"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "b3",
   "text": "But inside that theater, the seats were segregated. Black fans watched their own stars from a separate section.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "crowd",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "b4",
   "text": "Then the show sailed for England. It was a hit in London, and the company gave a command performance at Buckingham Palace.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "coons",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.1,
     1.2
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "b5",
   "text": "In Scotland, Bert became a Freemason. American lodges would not admit Black men.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "oval1903",
    "caption": "Bert Williams, 1903"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "b6",
   "text": "Back home, they followed it with Abyssinia, in 1906, and Bandanna Land, in 1908.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "bandanna",
    "caption": "Bandanna Land sheet music, 1907"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "b7",
   "text": "In those years Bert introduced the song that would follow him for the rest of his life. It was called Nobody.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "grin1905",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.05,
     1.15
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.45
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "b8",
   "text": "A sad, slow song about a man nobody ever helps. Audiences demanded it at almost every show.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "pk6",
    "focus": [
     0.7,
     0.3
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "a1",
   "text": "Then George Walker fell ill. In 1909 he collapsed on stage, and never performed again. He died in 1911.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "ww",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.1,
     1.2
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ]
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "1911"
   },
   "section": "alone"
  },
  {
   "id": "a2",
   "text": "Bert went on alone, in vaudeville. Many white performers refused to share a bill with him.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "bar3",
    "focus": [
     0.65,
     0.3
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "a3",
   "text": "On tour, he usually had to travel, eat and sleep apart from the rest of the company.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "suit1921b",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.2
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.05,
     1.15
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "z1",
   "text": "Then, in 1910, came an offer nobody expected. Florenz Ziegfeld wanted him for the Follies, the most famous revue on Broadway.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "palace",
    "caption": "Bert Williams poster, about 1918"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "1910"
   },
   "section": "follies"
  },
  {
   "id": "z2",
   "text": "Bert would be the only Black star in an all-white cast.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "bar1",
    "focus": [
     0.65,
     0.3
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "z3",
   "text": "Some of the cast gave Ziegfeld an ultimatum. Fire him, or we walk.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "lodge",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "z4",
   "text": "Ziegfeld refused. He reportedly told them: I can replace every one of you, except him.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "court1",
    "focus": [
     0.25,
     0.3
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "z5",
   "text": "When the show opened that June, Bert was the sensation of the night.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "pk2",
    "focus": [
     0.7,
     0.3
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "z6",
   "text": "Columbia Records signed him. His records sold all over the country, and he made dozens of them.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Columbia Records",
    "sub": "an exclusive contract"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "z7",
   "text": "He became one of the stars of the Follies for most of the next decade, alongside W. C. Fields, Fanny Brice, Will Rogers, and his close friend Eddie Cantor.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "follies1919",
    "caption": "Ziegfeld Follies of 1919, with Eddie Cantor"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "z8",
   "text": "He was the highest-paid Black entertainer in America.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "whitetie",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.2
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.05,
     1.15
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "i1",
   "text": "But fame did not open every door.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "bowler",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.2
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.05,
     1.15
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ]
   },
   "section": "insult"
  },
  {
   "id": "i2",
   "text": "When he came back to his hotel after a show, he was told to use the freight elevator.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "suit1921",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.22
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.1,
     1.2
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.36
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "i3",
   "text": "He told Eddie Cantor: It wouldn't be so bad, Eddie, if I didn't still hear the applause ringing in my ears.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "cell",
    "focus": [
     0.3,
     0.55
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.55
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "i4",
   "text": "Once, a bartender at a fancy New York bar tried to get rid of him. He said a drink would cost fifty dollars.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "bar2",
    "focus": [
     0.65,
     0.3
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "i5",
   "text": "Bert pulled out a pile of big bills, put it on the bar, and kept ordering.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "bar4",
    "focus": [
     0.65,
     0.3
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "i6",
   "text": "In his only known essay, he wrote that he had never found anything disgraceful in being a colored man. But he had often found it inconvenient. In America.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "tophat",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.15
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.05,
     1.15
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "f1",
   "text": "He also tried the movies. In 1913 he starred in a comedy with an all-Black cast, called Lime Kiln Field Day.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "limekiln",
    "caption": "Lime Kiln Field Day, 1913"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "1913"
   },
   "section": "film"
  },
  {
   "id": "f2",
   "text": "It was never released. The footage sat in a museum vault for a century, until the Museum of Modern Art restored it in 2014.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "limekiln",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.1,
     1.2
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.45
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "f3",
   "text": "In 1916, the Biograph company filmed him in a short comedy, A Natural Born Gambler. It survives.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "title",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.5
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.5
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "1916"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "f4",
   "text": "He plays the head of a lodge, who cannot stay away from the card table.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "table",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "f5",
   "text": "And at the end, locked in a cell, he performs his most famous routine.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "pk3",
    "focus": [
     0.7,
     0.3
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "f6",
   "text": "A poker game, with no cards, no table, and no other players. Just one man, under a light.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "pk5",
    "focus": [
     0.7,
     0.3
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "f7",
   "text": "You can follow every hand in his face. The hope, the bluff, the bad card, the loss.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "pk7",
    "focus": [
     0.7,
     0.3
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "n1",
   "text": "In August 1919, Broadway's actors went on strike. The whole Follies cast walked out.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "August 1919",
    "sub": "the actors' strike"
   },
   "section": "strike"
  },
  {
   "id": "n2",
   "text": "Nobody told Bert. He showed up for work, and found an empty theater.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "cell",
    "focus": [
     0.3,
     0.55
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.55
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.06,
     1.12
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "n3",
   "text": "After more than twenty years on the New York stage, the actors' union had never invited him to join.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "suit1921b",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.2
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.05,
     1.15
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "n4",
   "text": "Friends said he grew quieter, and sadder. W. C. Fields, who was close to him, said the strike helped break his health.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "pk8",
    "focus": [
     0.7,
     0.3
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "d1",
   "text": "By 1922 he was starring in a new show, Under the Bamboo Tree. He caught pneumonia, but kept performing.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "uniform",
    "caption": "Bert Williams"
   },
   "section": "end"
  },
  {
   "id": "d2",
   "text": "In February 1922, on tour in Detroit, he collapsed on stage.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "February 1922",
    "sub": "Detroit, Michigan"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "d3",
   "text": "At first, the audience thought it was part of the act.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "pk9",
    "focus": [
     0.7,
     0.3
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "d4",
   "text": "They helped him to his dressing room. He reportedly said: That's a nice way to die. They was laughing when I made my last exit.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "pk10",
    "focus": [
     0.7,
     0.3
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "d5",
   "text": "He was taken home to New York. On March 4th, 1922, Bert Williams died.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "uniform",
    "focus": [
     0.45,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.1,
     1.2
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.45,
     0.42
    ]
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "March 4, 1922"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "d6",
   "text": "Thousands of people lined up to see his coffin.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "court0",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "d7",
   "text": "And the Masons of New York, whose lodges had shut him out, gave him a Masonic funeral. He was the first Black man buried with Masonic honors.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "young1902",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.05,
     1.15
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "l1",
   "text": "His song Nobody outlived him by a century.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "pk6",
    "focus": [
     0.7,
     0.3
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.06,
     1.12
    ]
   },
   "section": "legacy"
  },
  {
   "id": "l2",
   "text": "Singers kept coming back to it for generations. In the year 2000, Johnny Cash recorded it.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Nobody",
    "sub": "Johnny Cash, 2000"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "l3",
   "text": "He spent his whole career behind a mask other people made for him. And he was still the best in the business.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "cork",
    "focus": [
     0.4,
     0.4
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.1,
     1.2
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.45,
     0.5
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "x1",
   "text": "He came from Nassau. He reached Broadway, London and the Follies. And every night, he painted on a face that wasn't his.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "suit1921",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.22
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.1,
     1.2
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.36
    ]
   },
   "section": "close"
  },
  {
   "id": "x2",
   "text": "That was Bert Williams. Subscribe for more.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "endcard"
   },
   "captions": false
  }
 ],
